 "questions": [
  {
   "n": 1,
   "type": "MCQ",
   "section": "Technical",
   "tags": "Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33",
   "html": "<p>How many order pairs of integer (a, b) are needed to guarantee that there are two ordered pairs<br />\n(<em>a</em><sub>1</sub>, b<sub>1</sub>) and (<em>a</em><sub>2</sub>, <em>b</em><sub>2</sub>) such that <em>a</em><sub>1</sub> mod 6 = <em>a</em><sub>2</sub> mod 6 and <em>b</em><sub>1</sub> mod 8 = <em>b</em><sub>2</sub> mod 8?</p>",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "html": "48"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "html": "49"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "html": "50"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "html": "52"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "B"
   ],
   "natRange": null,
   "answerText": "B",
   "marks": {
    "pos": 1,
    "neg": 0.33
   },
   "solution": "<p>(b)<br />\nFor a in (a, b) there different congruence class possible in mod 6 i.e. 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 8 different congruence classes possible for mod 8 i.e., 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8.<br />\nSo, number of different order pair where (<em>a</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>b</em><sub>1</sub>) and (a<sub>2</sub>, b<sub>2</sub>) such that a1 mod 6 = a<sub>2</sub> mod 6 and b<sub>1</sub> mod 8 = b<sub>2</sub> mod 8 not possible are = 6 &times; 8 = 48.<br />\nSo, to get two pair with given condition we need 48 + 1 = 49.</p>"
  },
